  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nStrong zonal flow aloft is in place across the region this \nafternoon. Daytime mixing and a potent mountain wave setup is \nallowing for some of the stronger winds and gusts aloft to be \ntranslated to the surface across the plains, particularly west of I-\n15. Mid-level flow in excess of 70 kts near the Rocky Mountain Front \nin and below the stable layer gives confidence in gusts greater than \n80 mph materializing in and around Browning as mountain waves ebb\nand flow east/west. Locally stronger gusts approaching 100 mph \ncannot be ruled out in wind prone locations near the immediate \neastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain Front through late afternoon.\nStronger surface winds decrease this evening across the plains. \n\nZonal flow aloft persists early this upcoming week, which will keep \nthe plains on the breezy side. A wave progressing through this flow \naloft will bring some light mountain precipitation late Monday night \ninto Tuesday, though snow levels largely look to remain above \n6,000ft. Cross barrier flow does increase with this wave, but by \nTuesday afternoon it trends a bit north of west, which is a less \nfavorable direction for resulting in High Wind criteria gusts. \nTrends will be monitored with this system. \n\nRidging builds in toward the latter portion of the week, which will \nallow for already mild temperatures to trend even warmer. \nUncertainty arises toward the weekend with respect to just how \nquickly the ridge is broken down. Additional windy stretches with \nlight mountain precipitation along the Continental Divide will be \nfavored when the ridge breakdown occurs. -AM\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds Today:\n\nLatest high resolution guidance has been consistent with the \nstrongest mountain wave retreating westward from the Cut Bank area \nlate this morning to the Browning area this afternoon. So far \nobservations support this trend, as areas near Browning have gusted \nup to 60 mph, while areas around Cut Bank have been gusting closer \nto 70 mph. The expectation is for the area of stronger gusts to \nshift westward this afternoon, with the overall magnitude of the \nwinds and gusts also increasing. A several hour stretch of sustained \nwinds in the 50 mph range, with frequent gusts between 75 and 85 mph \nis forecast in and around Browning this afternoon once the mountain \nwave retreats westward and becomes stationary. \n\nLate Monday night and Tuesday winds:\n\nAs mentioned prior, cross barrier flow increases once again as the \nnext wave progresses through the region. Orientation of the stronger \nflow aloft (southwesterly) is more favorable late Monday evening, \nthough ridgetop stability will be weaker compared to Tuesday. This \nraises doubts that stronger winds will materialize at the surface \nnear the Rocky Mountain Front Monday evening/night. As better \nridgetop stability develops into Tuesday the stronger winds aloft \ntrend more northwesterly. This orientation is less conducive to \nmixing stronger winds to the surface, but it will bear monitoring. \nAt this point the probability of a 65 mph gust in Browning is 50% \nTuesday. -AM",
